30. CONSTRAINT ENGINE
The geometry engine must not blindly execute parameters.
Before committing geometry, it evaluates active constraints.
Constraint classes include:
DIMENSIONAL
ALIGNMENT
DISTANCE
ANGLE
PARALLEL
PERPENDICULAR
COINCIDENT
CONTAINMENT
CLEARANCE
BOUNDARY
LEVEL
HOST
DEPENDENCY
CODE-DERIVED

31. HARD CONSTRAINTS
Hard constraints cannot be violated by an ordinary command.
Examples:
element must remain inside property boundary
opening must remain inside host wall
level elevation must remain valid
required clearance must remain satisfied
If a hard constraint fails:
COMMAND → REJECTED
No model revision is created.

32. SOFT CONSTRAINTS
Soft constraints may be violated only when explicitly permitted.
Example:
preferred room dimension
preferred alignment
preferred grid spacing
The system records the deviation.

33. CONSTRAINT CONFLICT
A conflict shall produce a structured result:
{
  "status": "CONFLICT",
  "constraint_id": "uuid",
  "object_id": "uuid",
  "severity": "ERROR",
  "message": "Requested movement violates required clearance.",
  "resolution_options": []
}
The geometry engine must not silently resolve a conflict by changing unrelated project geometry.

34. PARAMETRIC SOLVING
The initial solver shall support deterministic direct solving.
Example:
Wall A
    ↓
3000 mm

Wall B
    ↓
5000 mm

Room width
    ↓
Wall B - Wall A
Future versions may introduce a generalized constraint solver.

35. SOLVER PRIORITY
When multiple constraints affect an object, priority shall be:
1. Legal / code constraints
2. Project hard constraints
3. Structural constraints
4. Safety constraints
5. Explicit user constraints
6. Model relationships
7. Design preferences
8. Default geometry behavior
A lower-priority rule must not silently override a higher-priority rule.

